Remember the three R's?

43
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:58,160
Reduce, reuse, recycle.

44
00:02:58,160 --> 00:02:58,330
Exactly.

45
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:01,150
It's just small, simple changes.

46
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:04,370
Like buying energy-saving light bulbs.

47
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:06,530
Starting a compost bin.

48
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:08,470
Recycling bottles and papers.

49
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:10,410
Using reusable water bottles.

50
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:12,830
Stop using disposable cups and cutlery.

51
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:14,930
Like the ones we're drinking out of?

52
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,080
Yeah.

53
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:22,240
Alright, we're back.

54
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:26,120
So as you probably guessed, carbon footprint, it's pretty easy.

55
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:31,070
You're talking about a footprint is the evidence you leave if you're walking on sand, for example.

56
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:32,640
Exactly.

57
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:35,990
And if we talk about carbon, we're talking about the element, right?

58
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:37,680
Carbon.

59
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:38,680
Yep.

60
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:41,160
So carbon is something that is in everything, basically.

61
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:41,480
It's organic.

62
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:51,460
It can also, in the case of a carbon footprint, mean the amount of maybe waste or damage that we do to the earth or to the environment.

63
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:52,040
Right.

64
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:53,600
So for example, you're driving your car.

65
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:59,300
We all know that the gas that is emitted from the exhaust pipe is CO2, right?

66
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:00,520
And that contains carbon.

67
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:03,960
So that's a way of seeing that you're leaving a footprint of carbon.

68
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:07,280
You are damaging the atmosphere a little bit by driving your car.

69
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:15,360
So in talking about a carbon footprint, we talk about having a low carbon footprint, which is good, or a very high carbon footprint, which is bad.

70
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:17,240
Right.

71
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:19,880
So there were a lot of interesting things coming up in the dialogue.

72
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:21,360
One of the first things was...

73
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:29,340
Well, the first thing that they're talking about in this dialogue is they're having kind of an argument about whether or not global warming and global climate change really exists.

74
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:36,280
Yeah, this is a very interesting part because some people say, well, global warming is happening, global warming is real.

75
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:38,100
And other people say, well, it's not real.

76
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:39,580
It's not really a problem.

77
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,640
This is just a phase that the earth will go through and like everything, it'll disappear.

78
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:51,890
Well, I agree with people who say that the earth goes through phases because sometimes there are centuries that are hotter and sometimes there are centuries that are colder.

79
00:04:52,350 --> 00:05:00,600
But I think it's undeniable that there has been a large and immediate global climate change in the last maybe 20 years.

80
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:03,260
Yeah, we're definitely speeding up maybe that cycle, right?

81
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:03,620
Exactly.

82
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:12,340
And I think that's great evidence of this and terrible evidence of this is the three earthquakes, massive earthquakes that have happened in the last couple of months.

83
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:23,220
I think that earthquakes like last year and like this year are really, really important reminders that we do as humans influence the way that the world responds to us.

84
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:29,120
I think I always see it as the earth kind of just shaking its back, kind of saying, get off me.

85
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:29,820
Stop it.

86
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:31,600
Stop it.

87
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:32,600
That itches.

88
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:33,600
Yeah, exactly.

89
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:34,680
That's the way I see earthquakes sometimes.

90
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:38,960
But when they were talking about, well, is it real?

91
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:39,420
Is it not?

92
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:43,940
They use a really interesting phrase and says, it sounds like a bunch of mumbo jumbo.

93
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:45,220
Mumbo jumbo.

94
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:51,680
So this is a way to say that sounds like a bunch of garbage or it sounds like a bunch of gibberish.

95
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:55,920
It sounds like a bunch of people are talking about something that doesn't really exist.

96
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:59,720
So mumbo jumbo is basically something that's not really real or true.

97
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:06,200
I think don't you also, can you use it as somebody that's saying something you don't really understand?

98
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:06,660
Right.

99
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:15,560
So if maybe I'm talking to you about something that's very academic and very scholarly, you say, eh, sounds like a bunch of mumbo jumbo to me.

100
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:16,560
Exactly.

101
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:17,300
So that's what the person said.

102
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:21,480
Ah, you know, the environmental problems are a bunch of mumbo jumbo.

103
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:34,520
But it was really interesting because they were like, well, we have to save the earth and well, and then there was actually a phrase here that was really kind of hard for me to understand when the person said, I mean, honestly, we live in the boonies.

104
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:35,640
Boonies.

105
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:37,650
So what does it mean by living in the boonies?

106
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:42,130
This is a very common word to hear in spoken English, especially with young people.

107
00:06:42,690 --> 00:06:45,450
Boonies means we live far away from civilization.

108
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:47,270
We live far away from cities.

109
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:49,160
We live far away from everything.

110
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:57,770
So maybe these people live in the countryside or in a small village and they don't feel like they're affected by what people in the big cities do.

111
00:06:58,050 --> 00:06:58,430
Okay.

112
00:06:58,710 --> 00:07:03,790
So if you say, oh, I live in the boonies, you're just saying, I live really far, really far away.

113
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:05,730
So this is a very common American word.

114
00:07:05,870 --> 00:07:10,450
I think you can also say boondocks, which is like that movie Boondock Saints.

115
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:13,330
But boondocks and boonies means very far away.

116
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:17,310
So I could say, Marco, I'm not going to drive out to your house for a party.

117
00:07:17,530 --> 00:07:18,470
You live in the boonies.

118
00:07:19,010 --> 00:07:21,630
Do you have any other ways that you can say that you live really far away?

119
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:23,520
You live in Egypt.

120
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:28,020
You live in Egypt, which for us in America is obviously very, very far away.

121
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:31,700
Sometimes we say, oh, the youngs, exactly.

122
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:33,800
People in New Zealand say that.

123
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:37,840
I've also heard people say, oh, you live where the wind turns around.

124
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:38,560
Really?

125
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,000
I like that one.

126
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:41,460
It's like you live so far.

127
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:43,240
Oh God, you live where the wind turns around.

128
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:43,840
The end of the earth.

129
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:45,480
Exactly.

130
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:46,480
Wow.

131
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:48,080
So, well, there are a lot of different ways of saying that.

132
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:55,990
So they were talking about different aspects of the environment, like that we heard the word green or energy efficient.

133
00:07:56,510 --> 00:07:56,810
Okay.

134
00:07:56,830 --> 00:08:04,210
So basically what they're doing is they're talking about some of the things they've heard in the media, maybe, or on TV about what people are supposed to be doing.

135
00:08:05,070 --> 00:08:05,930
These are very abstract.

136
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:08,070
That means these are very kind of big terms.

137
00:08:08,950 --> 00:08:11,090
Go green or be more energy efficient.

138
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:12,360
Yeah.

139
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:16,790
I've heard this, like, for example, when people say, oh, you have to be more green or go green.

140
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:20,220
That means more friendly towards the ecosystem, right?

141
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:20,600
That's right.

142
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:30,920
So this is a very general suggestion and we can talk about specifics in a minute, but this is a very general suggestion for thinking about the environment and doing things to help it.

143
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:35,440
And so go green or be green is a way to be more environmentally friendly.

144
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:39,660
We also have using energy efficient computers or light bulbs or TVs.

145
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:45,200
The third thing that they talk about, and this is something that comes up a lot, is sustainability.

146
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:51,680
Yeah, that's a big word that a lot of people like to use in this type of topic, sustainability.

147
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:53,080
Right.

148
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:54,500
So sustainability comes from the word to sustain.

149
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:57,640
That means to hold out over time or to continue.

150
00:08:57,640 --> 00:09:10,520
So you think about something, maybe sustainability is, I think, in many cases about doing or having habits, doing things that you can continue to do for a long time.

151
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:17,100
For example, driving a big SUV to work every day is not keeping in mind sustainability because one day there will be no oil.

152
00:09:17,660 --> 00:09:18,260
So then what do we do?

153
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:19,660
Right.

154
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:20,720
So that's not a sustainable action.

155
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:25,200
Sustainable actions mean that we have less negative impact on the world.

156
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:26,200
Right.

157
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:34,400
So basically what you're trying to do is to sustain, which means to hold, to withstand these habits, for example.

158
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:43,100
So when you talk about a sustainable ecosystem, it means that it has everything it needs and it can continue to work for a very, very long time.

159
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:43,980
Right.

160
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:50,420
So you can also talk about sustainable development, which we talk about in aid and giving aid to other countries.

161
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:58,080
So sustainable development is trying to help other countries or other people in ways that they can continue to do for a long time.

162
00:09:58,080 --> 00:09:59,440
It's also very popular with tourism.

163
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:01,340
They talk about sustainable tourism.

164
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:02,000
Right.

165
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:10,240
That means thinking about the environment and having tourism that can last forever, not tourism that will destroy the environment because it's very harmful.

166
00:10:11,070 --> 00:10:12,670
It's a very interesting topic.

167
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:21,840
And as you say, it's used in a wide range of cases, sustainable development, sustainable projects, sustainable tourism.

168
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:25,090
There are a lot of different ways that people use this, but it's basically the same thing.

169
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:26,320
Right.

170
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:27,680
It's in essence, it refers to that.

171
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:28,680
Yes.

172
00:10:28,680 --> 00:10:29,170
Keeping something going over time.

173
00:10:29,830 --> 00:10:35,550
But I think the big question here is if you want to go green, if you want to be environmentally friendly, what do you do?

174
00:10:35,970 --> 00:10:38,690
Because it's not about big ideas.

175
00:10:38,870 --> 00:10:39,490
It's about specifics.

176
00:10:39,910 --> 00:10:41,610
It's about changing your everyday actions.

177
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:42,840
Right.

178
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:43,840
So these guys had a couple of ideas.

179
00:10:43,950 --> 00:10:44,070
Yeah.

180
00:10:44,210 --> 00:10:48,670
They came up with something like, for example, hybrid vehicles, which is a big thing now.

181
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:52,120
Basically hybrid means two, right?

182
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:54,250
It's a combination of two or more.

183
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:54,870
That's right.

184
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:57,880
So, you know the Sphinx in Egypt?

185
00:10:58,350 --> 00:10:58,770
That's a hybrid.

186
00:10:59,130 --> 00:10:59,250
Right.

187
00:10:59,350 --> 00:11:00,630
Between a man and a lion.

188
00:11:00,890 --> 00:11:01,130
Exactly.

189
00:11:02,240 --> 00:11:03,240
Right.

190
00:11:03,240 --> 00:11:07,240
So a hybrid vehicle basically uses gasoline, but also electricity.

191
00:11:07,270 --> 00:11:10,440
So they're actually very efficient because-

192
00:11:10,440 --> 00:11:11,440
And they're quiet.

193
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:12,720
They're quiet.

194
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:17,380
And usually where you use up most of your gasoline is in traffic.

195
00:11:17,380 --> 00:11:19,940
Because your car is on, you're accelerating, you're braking, you're accelerating, you're braking.

196
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:28,120
So in this case, I think hybrid vehicles run on the electric engine until they hit, I think it's 30 miles per hour.

197
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:39,240
And once you surpass that, then it switches to the gas engine, which obviously if you have a constant high speed, it's more efficient, the combustion of the gasoline.

198
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:40,240
Exactly.

199
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:43,200
So a good example of a hybrid vehicle is the Toyota Prius.

200
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:43,580
Right.

201
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:46,720
I think it was like the first one commercially available.

202
00:11:46,720 --> 00:11:50,000
But now they have all kinds and they're actually trying to make entirely electric cars.

203
00:11:50,920 --> 00:11:52,880
So lots of things to watch out for there.

204
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:55,380
But they also talked about something for the house, right?

205
00:11:55,600 --> 00:12:02,140
So if you want to not just have a nice car that is environmentally friendly, but also to do things with your house, what can you put on your roof?

206
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:04,160
I think we've seen a lot of these.

207
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:05,380
These are called solar panels.

208
00:12:06,260 --> 00:12:08,080
Solar has to do with the sun.

209
00:12:08,860 --> 00:12:12,940
So solar power, solar panel, that means energy that comes from the sun.

210
00:12:13,200 --> 00:12:14,060
So the sun.

211
00:12:14,620 --> 00:12:20,680
And a panel is like a pad or like some kind of device that can take the energy from the sun.

212
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:21,100
Yeah.

213
00:12:21,540 --> 00:12:22,420
It's really, really good.

214
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:27,740
Basically, in some places you have anywhere between eight to 12 hours of sunlight.

215
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:31,300
So the whole day, these panels are absorbing energy.

216
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:34,730
You can store them maybe in batteries and then just use them later.

217
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:43,880
So basically you don't really need to use electricity that comes from coal or from nuclear power plants because you want to turn on a light.

218
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:45,770
It doesn't really use up a lot of electricity.

219
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:46,670
And there's no waste.

220
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:47,470
And there's no waste.

221
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:49,240
It's all clean.

222
00:12:49,240 --> 00:12:53,430
So my question for you, does Ecuador have a lot of solar panels everywhere?

223
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:55,470
Because you guys have so much sunlight down there.

224
00:12:56,720 --> 00:12:58,890
We don't really have a lot of them.

225
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:07,590
It's not a big commercial thing yet, but there are a lot of projects going around to help implement these things a lot better.

226
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:17,200
For example, taking these technologies to the countryside where people don't have access to electricity by means of cables.

227
00:13:17,230 --> 00:13:20,960
So they're implementing that there, getting them to use it.

228
00:13:20,960 --> 00:13:22,260
But it's still expensive.

229
00:13:22,580 --> 00:13:27,540
That's the problem with solar panels is that, of course, it's an energy efficient way of going.

230
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:32,540
It'll save you money in the long run, but up front, you've got to spend a lot of money.

231
00:13:32,940 --> 00:13:34,420
Well, here's a good way to use sustaining.

232
00:13:34,820 --> 00:13:40,080
If someone in the countryside has solar panels in their house, it's a self-sustaining way to produce energy.

